## Burgundy & Deep Red Fall Nail Ideas

Some colour just owns a season, and burgundy has owned this one for longer than most trends survive. This year it has splintered into distinct directions worth knowing apart. Merlot leans purple-red and reads more editorial. Oxblood sits darker and browner, almost verging on black in low light. Cherry wine keeps more red in it and feels a touch warmer against most skin.

A single glossy coat of true wine red, no nail art at all, is still the version most people reach for, and it earns that reputation. Two coats gives full, saturated opacity; one thin coat over a nude base gives a sheer wash that photographs almost as well and wears lighter on shorter nails. If you want the colour with more dimension, a cat-eye gel finish in burgundy uses a magnetic top coat to pull a metallic line through the polish that shifts as your hand moves, giving the same wine tone a completely different personality after dark.

Nail art built on a burgundy base tends to work best when it stays restrained. A thin cream or gold micro-line at the tips reads considerably more current than a full opposite-colour French. Two accent nails in a sheer dusty rose glitter against an otherwise solid burgundy set is another way to break the colour up without losing the depth that makes it read fall in the first place. For anyone who wants a full spectrum of this shade family in one place, from merlot to oxblood to a near-black vamp, <a href='/blog/fall/burgundy-fall-nails'>burgundy nails for fall</a> covers it in more depth than a single section here can.

If you are matching a full look rather than just your hands, the same wine-toned family carries beautifully onto toes; <a href='/blog/fall/fall-pedicure-colors'>fall pedicure colors</a> is worth a look if that is on your list too.

